          "blockId": "BLK_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_1367A8D3_CONCEPTS_01_EQUILIBRIUM_MOMENTS",
          "semanticAnchorId": "ANCHOR_ROTATIONAL_EQUILIBRIUM",
          "title": "1. Mechanical Equilibrium and Principle of Moments",
          "contentMarkdown": "A rigid body is in equilibrium if both translational ($$\\\\sum \\\\mathbf{F} = 0$$) and rotational ($$\\\\sum \\\\boldsymbol{\\\\tau} = 0$$) conditions hold. The Principle of Moments states $$\\text{Load} \\\\times \\\\text{Load Arm} = \\text{Effort} \\\\times \\\\text{Effort Arm}$$. Mechanical advantage is $$MA = \\\\frac{F_{\\\\text{load}}}{F_{\\\\text{effort}}} = \\\\frac{d_{\\\\text{effort}}}{d_{\\\\text{load}}}$$.",
          "boundAtomicFactIds": [
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_1367A8D3_4DA53D85",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_1367A8D3_37A5FDCC",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_1367A8D3_C91AE3F9",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_1367A8D3_8C244587"
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "BLK_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_1367A8D3_CONCEPTS_02_ROTATIONAL_KINEMATICS",
          "semanticAnchorId": "ANCHOR_ROTATIONAL_KINEMATICS",
          "title": "2. Kinematics of Rotational Motion with Constant Acceleration",
          "contentMarkdown": "For constant angular acceleration $\\\\alpha$: $$\\omega = \\\\omega_0 + \\\\alpha t$$, $$\\theta = \\\\omega_0 t + \\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\alpha t^2$$, and $$\\omega^2 = \\\\omega_0^2 + 2\\\\alpha \\\\theta$$.",
          "boundAtomicFactIds": [
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_1367A8D3_2C707B98",
